From 2567ea4e312f26cbdf37ad9547206b437451992d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 12:55:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban scratch archive: never lazy-fetch; record
 promisor-only bases

Worker disk-pressure passes refused every aged blobless clone forever:

  ArchiveRefused: git diff --binary --no-ext-diff failed in .../repo (rc 128):
  fatal: could not read Username for 'https://github.com': terminal prompts
  disabled  fatal: could not fetch <oid> from promisor remote

Five whys:
1. Why refused? `git diff --binary HEAD` needs the HEAD-side blob of every
   changed path, and in a `--filter=blob:none` clone those blobs live only on
   the promisor remote, so git lazily fetched them.
2. Why did the fetch fail? The archiver's hardened env has no credential
   (global config is /dev/null, prompts off), so a private GitHub remote
   cannot be read. Where an askpass did work, it fetched one blob at a time
   over the network instead (a 26k-path tree ran past 300 s on worker-1).
3. Why so many changed paths? These are `--no-checkout` / failed-checkout
   clones: the index is empty, so every one of ~26k files diffs as a staged
   deletion whose base blob was never local.
4. Why never reclaimed? The refusal is deterministic; each pass retries the
   same trees and burns its budget.
5. Why no guard? The archiver assumed every object its diff needs is local.

Fix (hermes_cli/kanban_scratch_archive.py):
- Every archive git call runs offline: GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1 (git >= 2.44)
  plus `protocol.allow=never` appended to the hardened GIT_CONFIG_COUNT block
  (git 2.43 on the pool workers ignores the env var; with this any lazy fetch
  fails before connecting).
- For a partial clone (remote.*.promisor / extensions.partialclone, not
  false) with a HEAD: list changes with `git diff --raw` (no blob content),
  list HEAD's missing objects with `rev-list --objects --missing=print
  --no-walk HEAD` (never fetches). Paths whose base blob is missing go to
  remote_base.json (status, mode, oid, path); whatever the worktree still
  holds at those paths goes to remote_base.tar (symlinks as links). The
  binary diff runs only over the remaining paths (literal pathspecs, argv
  batches). With nothing missing the diff is exactly the old one.
- Custody: a missing base in a promisor repo is git's own promise that the
  remote holds it, so no local work is skipped; the worktree side is kept,
  unpushed commits still go to the verified bundle, and the complete
  filesystem payload (whole .git + worktree) is unchanged. A full clone,
  or one whose remote says promisor=false, with a missing object still
  refuses (it is corruption, not a promise).

Proof:
- RED on main: 3 new tests fail (no-checkout clone kept; archive fetched 2
  blobs from a reachable remote; env not offline).
- GREEN: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_scratch_archive_partial_clone.py (8)
  plus the scratch archive/custody/owned-jobs/preservation/lock-scope/
  trash-sweep/teardown/lockguard suites and the subprocess env/encoding
  scanners: 1570 passed.
- Mutants (sdlc-review mutants.py, 11): partial gate forced true/false,
  head guard dropped, promisor=false counted, literal pathspecs dropped,
  only the first batch, tar of absent paths, protocol not never, no
  lazy-fetch env, batch boundary, missing filter dropped: all killed.
- Real data, worker-1: the 14 local blobless trees whose diff failed
  without credentials now archive offline in 0.5-4.4 s each (26k remote-base
  entries for the no-checkout ones; one with an unpushed commit bundles it);
  every pack directory is unchanged afterwards (no fetch).
